Time Complexity:  O(q * n * log(min(a, b)))The gcd(a, b) function using Euclid’s algorithm takes O(log(min(a, b))) time. Since each lcm(a, b) call internally uses gcd, it also takes O(log(min(a, b))) time. For each type-2 query, we iterate over a subarray of size up to O(n) and compute LCM for each element, making each query cost O(n × log(min(a, b))). Therefore, for q queries, the overall time complexity becomes O(q × n × log(min(a, b))).Auxiliary Space: O(1) since we are only storing a few integers at a time. The space used by the input arr and queries is not considered.[Expected Approach] - Using Segment Tree - O(n + q × log n × log(min(a, b))) Time and O(n) SpaceThe idea is to use a Segment Tree to efficiently handle range LCM queries and updates. Instead of recomputing LCM for every query from scratch, we build a tree where each node stores the LCM of a segment of the array. This allows us to answer any range query by combining only the relevant segments (using LCM), and updates are handled by modifying a single element and updating only the affected nodes in the tree.
